{"description":"`discard` deletes a draft file permanently and irrecoverably, yet took no confirmation parameter: it wrote unconditionally. `transition` — a recoverable operation, since a work item can move forward again — was gated, with a refusal carrying the conformance report and an explicit retry affordance. The gate therefore protected the reversible operation and left the irreversible one exposed, an inverted safety gradient. Discard also frees the identity immediately, so discard-then-re-scaffold silently replaces authored content.","impact":"The most destructive MCP tool was the easiest to invoke by accident. Fix: `discard` refuses without `confirmed:true`, returning the same refusal shape transition uses (warning plus actionable retry), decided in the dispatch boundary so a refused call never reaches the capability layer and writes nothing. The refusal carries its own affordance wording, because the shared transition text speaks of a work item leaving its container, which would mislead an agent about what it is authorising."}